<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The dark gradient laid over the lower portion of a pet's photo card, so the name and pills
    written across it stay readable whatever the photograph underneath happens to be.

    **Why a gradient and not a flat panel.** A solid dark band would draw a hard horizontal line
    across every photo at the same height, which the eye reads as damage to the picture. Fading in
    from nothing lets the text sit on enough darkness to be legible while the photo appears to carry
    on behind it.

    **Why both ends are the same colour.** The transparent end is the *opaque* colour with a zero
    alpha channel, not #00000000. Android interpolates the whole ARGB value across the gradient, so
    fading from transparent black to a warm near-black would pass through grey on the way and show as
    a dirty band through the middle of the card. Matching the RGB makes only the alpha move.

    The angle is 270°, which in Android's convention runs top to bottom: clear at the top, darkest at
    the bottom edge where the pet's name sits.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">

    <gradient
        android:angle="270"
        android:endColor="@color/pet_card_scrim_opaque"
        android:startColor="@color/pet_card_scrim_transparent"
        android:type="linear" />

</shape>
